---
source: misc/tests/help.rs
expression: "help_output(\"findmnt\")"
---
List or find filesystems in /proc/self/mountinfo, /etc/fstab or /proc/swaps.

Reads the kernel's mount table by default. Use --fstab or --swaps to read the others. Filter with
--source, --target and --types, and pick columns with -o. Tree output (--tree) and the columns that
need statfs (SIZE, AVAIL, USED and USE%) are not implemented yet.

Usage: findmnt [OPTIONS] [DEVICE-OR-MOUNTPOINT]

Arguments:
  [DEVICE-OR-MOUNTPOINT]
          A target path or a source. The same as --target or --source, as in upstream findmnt

Options:
  -s, --fstab
          Read /etc/fstab instead of /proc/self/mountinfo

      --swaps
          Read /proc/swaps instead of /proc/self/mountinfo

  -k, --kernel
          Read /proc/self/mountinfo (the default)

  -F, --tab-file <FILE>
          Read mount table from this file instead of the source default

  -S, --source <SPEC>
          Filter to entries with a matching source (path or LABEL=/UUID=)

  -T, --target <PATH>
          Filter to entries with a matching target path

  -t, --types <LIST>
          Only show these filesystem types, separated by commas. Start the list with "no" to hide
          them instead (-t nofuse,nofusectl)

  -J, --json
          Use JSON output format

  -r, --raw
          Use raw output format

  -n, --noheadings
          Don't print headings

  -o, --output <LIST>
          Define which output columns to use

      --output-all
          Output all available columns

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
